<Doc
  title="Security"
  description="What a deployed mcpd stores, what leaves the machine and under which setting, how keys, scopes and project tenancy work, what redaction covers, and the controls to put around mcpd."
  lede="One page to answer a review. What is kept, what is sent anywhere else, who can read it, and the controls to put around mcpd."
  og="docs"
>
  <p>
    Everything below is drawn from the daemon's own source and from
    <code>spec/mcpd.spec.json</code>, which is what the API is generated from.
  </p>

  <h2>What mcpd stores</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd stores the text of your agent's conversations. That is the point of it,
    and a review should start from it. A span of kind
    <code>model_generation</code> carries <code>input</code> and
    <code>output</code>: the exact prompt sent to the model and the exact text
    that came back. For most users that is the most sensitive data they hold.
  </p>
  <p>Across the six signals, the stored free-form fields are:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Signal</th><th>Content it holds</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Spans</td>
        <td><code>input</code>, <code>output</code>, <code>attributes</code>, <code>metadata</code>, and the error message</td>
      </tr>
      <tr><td>Logs</td><td>the message, and the structured <code>fields</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Metrics</td><td><code>labels</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scores</td><td><code>reason</code>, and <code>metadata</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Feedback</td><td>the free-text <code>comment</code>, and <code>metadata</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Aspect notes</td><td>the sentence in <code>text</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Alongside the payload, every signal carries correlation fields that tie a run
    to the rest of your system: project, environment, service name and version,
    session, thread, user, resource, run, request, experiment and release.
    <code>user_id</code> is hashed on the way in unless you turn redaction off.
    The others are stored as sent, so treat any of them as a field a person
    could be identified by if you put an identity in one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Token counts and a cost figure are computed at ingest and stored with the
    run. Nothing about the model call is fetched from a provider.
  </p>

  <h3>Where it is written</h3>
  <p>
    One backend, chosen at start-up. The default is an embedded SQLite file at
    <code>./mcpd.db</code>, set with <code>--data</code>. The full-text index
    lives in the same file, so the store is one file to back up and one file to
    protect. <code>--postgres-url</code> or <code>--clickhouse-url</code> puts
    the data in a database you run instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    Durable runs, when they are on, live in a second SQLite file beside the
    first, whatever the trace store is. It keeps every run's input, state,
    output and correlation, and every step's input, output and error, exactly
    as they were sent: not redacted, with <code>user_id</code> unhashed,
    because a later step reads them back and a rewritten value would change
    what the run does. The spans a run writes into the trace store are
    redacted like any other. Protect the run file as you protect the trace
    store, or more so. Retention and erasure by subject both reach it, for
    runs that have ended; see <a href="/docs/runs#storage" class="link">Runs</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Encryption at rest is the volume's job
    on the embedded store and the database's job on the other two.
  </p>

  <h2>What leaves the machine</h2>
  <p>
    With a default configuration, nothing. The daemon makes outbound requests in
    exactly four places, and every one of them is off until you configure it.
    There is no update check, no usage reporting and no telemetry of any kind.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Destination</th><th>What is sent</th><th>Off unless</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Storage backend</td>
        <td>Everything mcpd stores, over your own connection.</td>
        <td>You set <code>--postgres-url</code> or <code>--clickhouse-url</code>. The default store is a local file and needs no network at all.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Forwarding</td>
        <td>
          Every stored span, re-emitted as OTLP JSON to
          <code>&#123;endpoint&#125;/v1/traces</code>. This includes the payload:
          <code>input</code> and <code>output</code> go out as the
          <code>input.value</code> and <code>output.value</code> attributes,
          each capped at 32 KiB. Values already redacted stay redacted, and a
          hashed <code>user_id</code> is forwarded as the hash.
        </td>
        <td>You set <code>--forward-endpoint</code>, or a <code>forward</code> block in the config file.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Monitor and objective webhooks</td>
        <td>
          A fixed JSON body: the monitor or objective id and name, the project,
          the condition, what it watches, the threshold, the reading, the sample
          count, a one-line detail and a timestamp. No prompt or completion text
          is in it.
        </td>
        <td>A monitor or objective in the config file names a <code>webhook</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Judge model</td>
        <td>
          The criterion, plus the run's question and its answer, each truncated
          at 4,000 characters, POSTed to
          <code>&#123;endpoint&#125;/chat/completions</code>. This is the one path
          that sends stored prompt and completion text to a model provider.
        </td>
        <td>A <code>judge</code> block in the config file names an endpoint, a model and at least one criterion, and a caller asks for that scorer by name.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    The interface the binary serves at <code>/</code> is compiled into it. Its
    fonts, styles and scripts come from the same origin, and it makes no
    third-party requests.
  </p>

  <h2>Keys and scopes</h2>
  <p>
    Authentication is API keys. By default there is no account, no session and
    no login; <code>--signup</code> adds them, as described below. A
    key is presented as <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code> or in the
    <code>X-Mcpd-Key</code> header, and both sides are hashed with SHA-256
    before they are compared, so neither the length of the configured key nor
    the position of the first wrong character is observable from timing.
  </p>
  <p>There are six scopes, in order of the reach they grant:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Scope</th><th>What it permits</th><th>Why it is its own scope</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ingest</code></td>
        <td>Write signals. Read nothing.</td>
        <td>This key lives in every container that emits a span, including the ones running third-party code. It must not also open every stored prompt.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>read</code></td>
        <td>Read stored signals.</td>
        <td>Reading is where the prompts are.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>write</code></td>
        <td>Change stored data: scores, feedback, datasets, experiments and acknowledgements.</td>
        <td>Several of those handlers read a payload on the way through. Capturing a production run into a dataset reads that run's input and output, which is more than emitting a span.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>execute</code></td>
        <td>Start, cancel and signal runs, and publish events to them.</td>
        <td>Starting a run spends money and reaches into your systems. A key that may record a score should not also be able to launch work.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>worker</code></td>
        <td>Claim steps and report what happened to them.</td>
        <td>This is the key inside the process that runs step bodies. A claim hands back the step's input and every earlier step's output, so it reads payloads, and it should not also be able to start runs or change stored data.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>admin</code></td>
        <td>Destroy data, and read the audit log.</td>
        <td>Deleting is not reading.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Which scope an endpoint takes is declared per endpoint in the specification
    and published in the generated
    <a href="/docs/reference/api" class="link">HTTP API</a> reference, so there
    is one answer to "what does this endpoint need" rather than one per document.
  </p>

  <h3>Accounts, with --signup</h3>
  <p>
    With <code>--signup</code>, anyone may create an account with an email and a
    password. Each account owns one project with a random name. Logging in gives
    a session token that holds every scope on that project, and the account can
    mint API keys for it, one scope each. Neither reaches any other project. A
    signal an account key sends is stored in the account's project, whatever
    project it names, and an idempotency key is kept per project, so one
    account cannot make another's batch look like a replay. Only a session can
    list, mint or revoke keys, so a key that leaks cannot make more.
  </p>
  <p>
    Passwords are hashed with argon2id. Tokens and keys are random 32-byte
    values, shown once, and stored only as SHA-256 digests. A wrong password
    and an unknown email get the same answer. Sign-up and log-in are limited
    per client address, and failed log-ins per email. Sessions last 30 days.
  </p>
  <p>
    The mode also turns off the rule that a scope with no keys is open: a
    request with no credential is refused everywhere except the probes,
    sign-up and log-in. Configured keys are tried first and keep the reach
    they have. See <a href="/docs/configuration#signup" class="link">Open sign-up</a>
    for the flags.
  </p>

  <h3>The fallback, and what it means for a key you already have</h3>
  <p>
    An unset <code>write</code> list falls back to the <code>ingest</code> list,
    and an unset <code>admin</code> list falls back to the <code>read</code>
    list. Unset <code>execute</code> and <code>worker</code> lists fall back to
    the <code>write</code> list, and so to the <code>ingest</code> list when
    that is unset too. That is what stops an upgrade from revoking access on
    every deployment configured before the narrower scopes existed. An admin key
    is also accepted for <code>execute</code> and <code>worker</code>, so an
    operator can drive a run by hand. Together, these mean that on a daemon
    holding only an ingest key and a read key:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the ingest key also attaches scores and feedback, starts runs and claims steps, and</li>
    <li>the read key also deletes traces and, through the admin fallback, starts runs and claims steps.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    So a read key is a delete key until you set <code>--admin-key</code>, and
    both keys reach the run surface until you set <code>--execute-key</code>,
    <code>--worker-key</code> and <code>--admin-key</code>. Hand keys out on
    that basis. Nothing narrows on its own. Each scope has a flag,
    <code>--ingest-key</code>, <code>--read-key</code>,
    <code>--write-key</code>, <code>--admin-key</code>,
    <code>--execute-key</code> and <code>--worker-key</code>, and each takes a
    list; every one of them can also be set per project in the config file.
  </p>

  <h3>With no keys, every scope is open</h3>
  <p>
    That default is deliberate: a first run on a laptop must not need a
    credential. It is also the single most dangerous thing about an exposed
    instance, so when the daemon binds a non-loopback address, it logs a
    warning at start-up for each surface open to a caller that should not reach
    it. Each warning names the surface and the flags that close it. Run
    <code>mcpd config</code> before you expose a port: it prints the
    configuration an invocation resolves to, then exits. It carries a line per
    scope, and it names the list a scope has fallen back to, so
    <code>admin auth      key required (read keys)</code> tells you the read
    keys can still delete.
    <a href="/deploy" class="link">Docker and Kubernetes</a> shows the output.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    label="closed before it is reachable"
    command={'mcpd --ingest-key "$INGEST" --read-key "$READ"'}
    primaryLabel="Flags"
    secondary={[
      { label: 'All six scopes', command: 'mcpd --ingest-key "$I" --read-key "$R" --write-key "$W" --execute-key "$E" --worker-key "$K" --admin-key "$A"' },
      { label: 'As environment', command: 'MCPD_INGEST_KEYS=… MCPD_READ_KEYS=… mcpd' },
      { label: 'Check first', command: 'mcpd config' },
    ]}
  />

  <h3>A project is a tenancy boundary</h3>
  <p>
    A key configured at the top level grants every project. A key listed instead
    under a named project in the <code>projects</code> block of the config file
    grants that project and no other, and each project holds its own list per
    scope. One key may appear under several projects, which is how a platform
    team holds a key spanning what it operates without holding the whole server.
  </p>
  <p>On the write path the check is per signal, not per request:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A batch may name several projects. Signals naming a project the key does
      not reach are dropped and reported in the response as rejected, with the
      reason, so a fleet pointed at the wrong project is discoverable from the
      response it already reads. The rest of the batch is stored.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On the read path, every endpoint that names a project checks it against the
    key's grant: traces, spans, logs, metrics, scores, feedback, analytics,
    datasets, experiments and the audit log. The items inside a dataset are
    addressed by the dataset's id, and the daemon looks up which project owns
    that dataset and checks the key against it, so a key that cannot open the
    project cannot read the cases captured from it. An id that does not exist
    is refused the same way as one the key may not reach, so the endpoint
    cannot be used to test which ids exist. Three endpoints take no project and are reachable by any key holding the
    read scope, whatever projects it is scoped to: the project list, the scorer
    catalogue and the storage capability set
    (<code>GET /v1/api/capabilities</code>). The project list is filtered by
    the key's grant, so a project-scoped key sees only the projects it reaches.
    On a shared instance, project names are often team or customer names. The
    monitor state and the objective state were on that list until they were
    filtered by the key's project grant, which they should always have been: a
    monitor names the project it watches and carries the webhook it posts to.
    The API names only the host of that webhook, because a webhook URL is often its own credential.
  </p>

  <h3>A refusal says which of the two problems it is</h3>
  <p>
    A missing or unrecognised key is 401, and a recognised key that this
    endpoint or this project does not admit is 403. The split is operational: a
    401 sends you to look for a typo in a credential, a 403 tells you the
    credential is right and the grant is wrong.
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="four refusals"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'curl -sw "%{http_code} " "$MCPD/v1/api/traces?project=acme"' },
      { out: '401  this endpoint needs an API key; send it as `Authorization: Bearer <key>`', tone: 'warn' },
      { cmd: 'curl … -H "authorization: Bearer $TYPO"' },
      { out: '401  that key is not one this daemon knows', tone: 'warn' },
      { cmd: 'curl … -H "authorization: Bearer $INGEST"' },
      { out: '403  that key is not valid for reading', tone: 'warn' },
      { cmd: 'curl … -H "authorization: Bearer $BILLING_READ"' },
      { out: '403  that key does not reach the project "acme"', tone: 'warn' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    That last message is the same whether the project exists or not. Confirming
    which project names are real is a small leak, and on a shared daemon those
    names are the names of your customers.
  </p>

  <h3>The audit log</h3>
  <p>
    Handing a stored payload back out of mcpd is recorded, and so is every
    deletion, so the question "who read this run" has an answer. A row carries
    when, which key, what action, which project, which trace or dataset it
    named, and whether the request was served or refused.
    <code>GET /v1/api/audit</code> reads it back, newest first, and takes the
    <code>admin</code> scope.
  </p>
  <p>
    Eighteen actions are recorded: <code>get_trace</code>, <code>list_spans</code>,
    <code>list_logs</code>, <code>list_feedback</code>,
    <code>list_dataset_items</code>, <code>capture_into_dataset</code>,
    <code>read_trace_aspect_notes</code>, <code>get_theme</code>,
    <code>get_theme_flow</code>, <code>list_theme_history</code>,
    <code>list_theme_examples</code>,
    <code>delete_trace</code>, <code>erase_subject</code>, and five on durable
    runs: <code>list_runs</code>, <code>get_run</code>,
    <code>list_run_events</code>, <code>get_step_cause</code> and
    <code>cancel_run</code>. The boundary is the payload, not the request.
    Listing traces returns a projection of names, timings, token counts and
    cost with no prompt or completion in it, so it is not recorded; opening one
    of those traces is, because that is where the text is. Listing durable runs
    is recorded, because every run in the list carries its input, output and
    state as they were sent. A refused read of a run is recorded under the
    project that owns the run.
  </p>
  <p>
    The worker calls are not recorded: claiming a step, declaring it started,
    the heartbeat, complete and fail. A lease does carry the step's input and
    the outputs of the steps before it, but the worker loop claims many times a
    minute, and a row per claim would bury the reads a person made. Treat the
    <code>worker</code> key as one that reads every payload of the runs it
    serves. Starting a run is not recorded either; a repost of a held
    idempotency key answers with the run that key started, so treat the
    <code>execute</code> key as one that can read the runs it starts.
  </p>
  <p>Five properties a reviewer should know:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The key is never written down.</strong> The principal is a
      truncated SHA-256 digest of the key, which distinguishes one key from
      another without storing a verifier for it. A request with no key on an
      open daemon is recorded as <code>anonymous</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The person comes from your ingress.</strong> On its own a row
      attributes an action to a key. The <code>subject</code> field carries an identity
      when <code>--trusted-identity-header</code> and
      <code>--trusted-proxy-cidr</code> are both set, and is empty otherwise,
      which is the default. mcpd never establishes that identity itself; it
      records what an address you named asserted.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A refusal is recorded too.</strong> The row goes down before the
      403 is returned, because a refused attempt on another team's project is
      exactly the row an audit log exists for. Reading the log is not itself
      recorded: it would grow without a payload ever being read, and the row
      would say nothing the holder of the admin key does not already know.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It records which key, and optionally who.</strong> The key is
      stored as a truncated digest, never the key itself, so the log is not a
      place credentials accumulate. That answers "an admin key deleted this".
      To answer "which person did", give it the header
      your ingress already sets and the addresses allowed to set it:
      <code>--trusted-identity-header x-forwarded-user</code> with
      <code>--trusted-proxy-cidr 10.42.0.0/16</code>. Both halves are required.
      A header is a string anyone who can reach the port may send, so mcpd
      reads it only from an address you named; a request from anywhere else is
      served normally and its row carries no subject rather than a
      guessed one. With neither flag set, no subject is ever
      recorded, and that is the default.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Retention does not trim it.</strong> The retention pass removes
      spans, logs, metrics, traces, scores, feedback, aspect notes and ended
      durable runs. It leaves
      the audit log, because a log that expired alongside the data it describes
      could not say who read the deleted data.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It never fails the request it describes.</strong> A row that
      cannot be written is logged and counted as
      <code>mcpd_audit_write_failures_total</code> on <code>/metrics</code>, and
      the read still returns. An audit log that can take down the read path is a
      worse liability than the one it prevents, so watch that counter rather
      than relying on requests failing.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What no key covers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The interface.</strong> The markup at <code>/</code> is served to
      anyone who can reach the port. The data behind it is not: every request it
      makes goes through the query API and meets the same keys. There is no
      login screen in the daemon unless <code>--signup</code> is on.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The probes and <code>/metrics</code>.</strong> Unauthenticated on
      purpose, because a kubelet and a scraper cannot carry a key. They report
      status, version, uptime, the storage backend's name and counters, never
      payloads.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Origin.</strong> The query API and the probes answer with
      <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *</code>, so a browser page on any
      origin may call them. mcpd uses no cookies and no ambient credentials, so
      such a page still has to hold a key: a permissive origin policy does not
      hand it one.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What redaction does</h2>
  <p>
    Redaction is on by default and runs before the write, as stage three of the
    intake pipeline. A value a rule matches never reaches the database, which is
    a different guarantee from a value deleted afterwards, and the difference is
    the one an auditor asks about. To keep the bytes off the network as well,
    the SDKs carry <code>MCPD_CAPTURE_PAYLOADS=false</code>, which drops
    <code>input</code> and <code>output</code> before a span is queued and
    leaves everything else on it.
  </p>

  <PipelineDiagram class="not-prose my-8" />

  <p>
    A rule is a set of lowercase substrings and a mode. A key matches when its
    name, lowercased, contains one of them. Matched values are masked, partially
    masked, hashed with a keyed digest, or dropped. When a matched key holds an
    object, everything under it is redacted too, whatever the child keys are
    called, and a secret sent as a JSON number is treated the same as one sent
    as a string. The full rule table is on
    <a href="/docs/privacy" class="link">Redaction and retention</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Rules match field names, not the shape of a value.</strong> A value
    that arrives under a field called <code>content</code> or
    <code>question</code> is stored as sent, so name the fields that carry
    credentials and identifiers.
  </p>
  <p>Redaction covers a precise scope:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>It stops the structured capture of credentials and identifiers, where a credential arrives in a field named after what it is.</li>
    <li>It works on field names. Free text under an ordinary field name is stored as sent.</li>
    <li>It leaves a span's name and a log record's message as sent. Those are identifiers used for grouping rather than payloads, and rewriting them would break the grouping. Do not build a span name out of a customer's email address.</li>
    <li>It leaves the run store as sent. A durable run's payloads are its working state; only the spans a run writes into the trace store are redacted.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One control works on shape, as detection after the fact: the built-in
    <code>no_leaked_secret</code> scorer marks down a run whose
    <em>answer</em> carries a token that looks like a credential, by known
    prefix or by a long mixed-case alphanumeric run. It scores the answer of a
    run that is already stored, removes nothing, and runs only when you ask for
    it. Use it to find out that this has happened, not as a second line of
    redaction.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two bounds apply whether or not a rule matched. Any single string longer
    than 128 KiB is truncated on a character boundary with a marker, and walking
    stops at a depth of 32. Both exist so one runaway document cannot decide the
    size of your database or pin a CPU.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--no-redact</code> turns the whole pipeline off, which is sensible
    only when something upstream already redacts. Where redaction hashes an
    identifier, set <code>--redact-key</code>: the built-in key is a constant in
    published source, and identifiers come from small spaces, so an unkeyed
    digest can be recovered by guessing candidates.
  </p>

  <h2>Retention and erasure</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--retention-days</code> deletes signals older than that many days.
    Omit it and nothing is ever deleted, which is the right default: a tool that
    quietly discarded last month's traces would be discovered at the worst
    moment.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pass runs on an hourly timer inside the daemon, never inside a user's
    request. It covers every project named in the <code>projects</code> block,
    each with its own <code>retention_days</code> where it sets one and the
    global figure where it does not, plus the default project. A project with no
    window anywhere is not trimmed at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a request about one person,
    <code>DELETE /v1/api/subjects/{'{user_id}'}?project=…</code> removes every
    run recorded against that person in the project, with the spans, logs,
    metrics, scores and feedback attached to them, and answers with a count per
    signal. The identifier is hashed with the same key before it is matched, so
    the request works against hashed identifiers without you hashing anything
    yourself, and the response says how many stored identifiers were in a form
    the request could not match. To remove one run rather than one person,
    <code>DELETE /v1/api/traces/{'{trace_id}'}</code> removes it and everything
    attached to it in one transaction. Both take the <code>admin</code> scope
    and both are recorded in the audit log.
    <a href="/docs/privacy" class="link">Redaction and retention</a> walks
    through the erasure request and what its reply tells you.
  </p>

  <h2>Controls to put around mcpd</h2>
  <p>
    These controls belong to the layer around the daemon. Each row names where
    to set it.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Control</th><th>Where to set it</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>TLS.</strong> mcpd serves plain HTTP, and prompts and completions travel over that link in full.</td>
        <td>Terminate TLS at your ingress or in your mesh, and keep the daemon's port off any untrusted network.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Single sign-on.</strong> A person's identity comes from the proxy in front of mcpd.</td>
        <td>Put the interface behind the identity proxy you already run. A person's identity is whatever that proxy establishes.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Provisioning.</strong> Inside mcpd the unit is a key. The accounts <code>--signup</code> creates own one project each.</td>
        <td>Provision at the proxy. Inside mcpd, add a key to a list or remove it.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Roles.</strong> Authorisation is one of six scopes and a project list.</td>
        <td>Separate by key and by project. If a distinction matters, make it a project.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Who a person is.</strong> The audit log records the subject your ingress asserted, and only from an address you named.</td>
        <td>Set <code>--trusted-identity-header</code> and <code>--trusted-proxy-cidr</code> so the header your proxy already sets reaches the audit row. Without them a row attributes an action to a key, and issuing one key per team narrows the digest.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Key lifecycle.</strong> Keys are lists read at start-up. The ceiling is per project: <code>--quota-per-minute</code> bounds how many signals a project may store in a minute, whichever key sent them.</td>
        <td>Configure several keys, one per team or fleet, so revoking one is an edit and a restart rather than a redeployment of everything that emits a span. Make a fleet its own project if it needs its own ceiling.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Encryption at rest.</strong> mcpd writes plain files or plain rows.</td>
        <td>Encrypt the volume, or use a database that does it.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Reporting something</h2>
  <p>
    Suspected vulnerabilities go to <strong>hi@mcpd.sh</strong> with "Security"
    in the subject, not to a public issue. The repository's
    <code>SECURITY.md</code> states the scope and what a good report contains.
    An unauthenticated instance that somebody chose to expose is a documented
    default rather than a finding. Anything that gets past a key that
    <em>was</em> configured, past a configured redaction rule, or across a
    project boundary is one.
  </p>

